**Definition 1.2 (the actual double-wall source).** Fix

$$
0<Z<1,
\qquad r=1-Z,
\qquad c=r/2,
\qquad b=(c,c,Z).
\tag{QC.1}
$$

Mode order is $(0,1,2,3,13)$; index $4$ denotes mode $13$. The strict
probability coordinate is $0<k<c$, and the supplied probabilities and signed
weights become

$$
\begin{aligned}
p(k)&=(k,c-k,Z,c-k,k),\\
w(k)&=\left(\frac12-\frac{k}{r},-\frac{k}{r},0,
                 -\frac{k}{r},\frac12-\frac{k}{r}\right).
\end{aligned}
\tag{QC.2}
$$

An original control has finite duration $T\geq0$, continuous real $k,\chi$
on $[0,T]$, strict $k(t)\in(0,c)$, finite variation of $\chi$, and either
absolute continuity of both coordinates or the supplier's finite piecewise
$C^1$ alternative. In the latter alternative both derivative rates extend
continuously to each closed partition piece. The supplied fundamental
theorem argument makes both coordinates absolutely continuous on the full
interval. Repeated partition times are allowed. These are the original two
regularity alternatives, not an extra common speed assumption.

Fix calibrated initial phases $\theta_i\in\mathbb R$, with $\theta_2=0$,
and a fixed positive preparation. The actual phase increment on a prescribed
control is

$$
H_i(t)=\int_0^t w_i(k(u))\chi'(u)\,du.
\tag{QC.3}
$$

Real lifts are retained. For a based loop, the real increment of $\chi$ is
$2\pi m$ for an arbitrary signed $m\in\mathbb Z$. Reduction of an endpoint
phase modulo $2\pi$ does not remove a source history or its integer winding.
At a chronological join the actual tail has lift
$\chi(T)+\chi_{\rm tail}(u)-\chi_{\rm tail}(0)$, and the probability
endpoints match. The cited composition declaration preserves the old prefix
pointwise, identifies this literal tail, and adds all the weighted integrals.
It includes zero duration. Translation of a tail does not give the controller
the unknown actual past lift.

